sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "If randomness in an experiment comes from intrinsic generation, then even the precise details of the random-looking behavior should be repeatable from one run to the next, because such behavior is robust to perturbations below a critical size, whereas randomness from the environment or from initial conditions should show little or no repeatability.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Intrinsically generated randomness is repeatable across experimental runs";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Intrinsic Generation of Randomness” (from p. 315), p. 323. Scope: carefully controlled experiments showing random-looking behavior"
}
